            <ArticleTitle>'Validation of ultrasound examinations performed by general practitioners'.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>256-261</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/02813432.2017.1358437</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to evaluate the diagnostic agreement when a general practitioner and subsequently a specialist (radiologist/gynecologist) performed point-of-care ultrasound examinations for certain abdominal and gynecological conditions of low to moderate complexity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A prospective study of inter-rater reliability and agreement.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients were recruited and initially scanned in general practice. The validation examinations were conducted in a hospital setting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A convenient sample of 114 patients presenting with abdominal pain or discomfort, possible pregnancy or known risk factors toward abdominal aortic aneurism were included.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Inter-rater agreement (Kappa statistic and percentage agreement) between ultrasound examinations by general practitioner and specialist for the following conditions: gallstones, ascites, abdominal aorta &gt;5 cm, intrauterine pregnancy and gestational age.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">An overall Kappa value of 0.93 (95% confidence interval (CI): 0.87-0.98) was obtained. Ascites, abdominal aortic diameter &gt;5cm, and intrauterine pregnancy showed Kappa values of 1.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our study showed that general practitioners performing point-of-care ultrasound examinations with low-to-moderate complexity had a very high rate of inter-rater agreement compared with specialists.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
